<h2>Rectangles with the Same Area</h2>
<p><a href="https://cms-assets.illustrativemathematics.org/h9ajs44azmw9zbyxcquhtbcum4ne" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open the four-page original 2021 student PDF</a>. The source diagrams are publisher-linked, not embedded.</p>
<p>Number Talk: 40÷4=10, 60÷4=15, 80÷4=20, 96÷4=24. Explain 96÷4 as 80÷4 + 16÷4.</p>
<table class="source-chart"><thead><tr><th>Source area (square units)</th><th>Example A</th><th>Example B with different perimeter</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>24 (Activity 1)</td><td>2×12: perimeter28</td><td>4×6: perimeter20</td></tr>
<tr><td>12</td><td>2×6: perimeter16</td><td>3×4: perimeter14</td></tr>
<tr><td>20</td><td>2×10: perimeter24</td><td>4×5: perimeter18</td></tr>
<tr><td>42</td><td>3×14: perimeter34</td><td>6×7: perimeter26</td></tr>
<tr><td>48</td><td>4×12: perimeter32</td><td>6×8: perimeter28</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>Original Activity 1 asks you to DRAW as many area-24 rectangles as fit on its smaller grid and calculate perimeter. For Activity 2 draw two equal-area but different-P rectangles for 12/20/42/48 and your own area. To check only the numeric dimensions, type <code>a,b;c,d</code>.</p>
<p>Use original standard dot paper, whose dimensions allow 19 by 24 intervals. Cut shapes, display on labelled area posters, compare and discuss: teacher must review actual physical work. No computer score replaces this. Publisher Cool-down remains gated.</p>
